.TH TAR 1 2026-10-27 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
tar \- create, list, and extract tar archives
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tar \-c
.RI [ options ]
.IR file ...
.br
.B tar \-t
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ member ...]
.br
.B tar \-x
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ member ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tar
creates an archive of files and directories, lists the members of an
archive, or extracts them.
This is the busybox
.BR tar .
Exactly one of
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-t ,
and
.B \-x
chooses the mode.
Giving none, or more than one, prints a usage message and fails.
.PP
The archive is standard input for
.B \-t
and
.BR \-x ,
and standard output for
.BR \-c ,
unless
.B \-f
names a file.
Standard input and output are in binary mode.
A file written by
.B \-c
is created, or truncated if it exists.
.PP
The first argument may be option letters with no leading dash, as in
.BR "tar czf out.tgz dir" .
Then
.B f
takes the next argument wherever it stands among the letters.
Use dashes when other letters, such as
.BR C ,
take arguments.
.SS Compression
.B tar
does not compress data itself.
It runs a separate program with the arguments
.BR "\-cf \-" ,
so that the program reads standard input and writes standard output.
When creating an archive,
.BR \-z ,
.BR \-j ,
.BR \-J ,
.BR \-\-lzma ,
.BR \-\-zstd ,
and
.B \-Z
run
.BR gzip ,
.BR bzip2 ,
.BR xz ,
.BR lzma ,
.BR zstd ,
and
.BR compress .
When reading, they run
.BR gunzip ,
.BR bunzip2 ,
.BR unxz ,
.BR unlzma ,
.BR unzstd ,
and
.BR uncompress .
If several of these options are given, the one used is the earliest in
this list, whatever their order on the command line:
.BR \-\-zstd ,
.BR \-J ,
.BR \-\-lzma ,
.BR \-j ,
.BR \-z ,
.BR \-Z .
.PP
.BR bzip2 ,
.BR bunzip2 ,
.BR unxz ,
.BR unlzma ,
and
.B uncompress
are busybox applets, and run as busybox itself.
The other programs are found in
.BR PATH ,
and so are
.B xz
and
.B lzma
when compressing, since the applets cannot compress.
A program that cannot be started is reported as
.BI "can't execute '" name "'"
and the status is 1.
.PP
In the kit,
.B gzip
and
.B gunzip
are tugz, and
.B zstd
and
.B unzstd
are Zstandard.
The kit has no
.BR compress ,
and its
.B xz
and
.B lzma
only decompress, so
.BR \-Z ,
.BR \-J ,
and
.B \-\-lzma
fail when creating an archive unless
.B PATH
has a program that can compress, ahead of the kit's.
A failed compression leaves an empty or partial archive file.
.PP
.B tar
cannot pass options to the program.
To choose a compression level, compress its output instead.
When reading, the exit status of the decompressor is ignored, and a
damaged stream shows up as an error in the archive, such as
.BR "short read" .
.PP
When listing or extracting without a decompression option,
.B tar
looks at the first block of the archive.
If it is not a tar header,
.B tar
recognizes gzip, bzip2, xz, zstd, and compress data by its first bytes,
and decompresses it as above.
This needs a seekable archive: a named file, or standard input
redirected from a file.
Compressed data from a pipe needs a decompression option, or
.B tar
fails with
.BR "invalid tar magic" .
A file named by
.B \-f
whose name ends in
.B .lzma
is decompressed as lzma data, which has no signature to recognize.
.SS Member names
With no operands,
.B \-t
and
.B \-x
use every member.
Otherwise each
.I member
is a shell wildcard pattern, matched case-sensitively against the names
in the archive, including a leading
.BR ./ ,
once leading slashes and
.B ../
components are removed as described under Extracting.
A trailing
.B /
on the pattern is removed, and a backslash quotes the next character.
A pattern with
.I n
slashes is compared with the first
.IR n +1
components of a name, so
.B dir
selects
.B dir
and everything below it, and
.B dir/*.c
selects
.B dir/x.c
but not
.BR dir/sub/x.c .
The patterns of
.B \-X
and
.B \-\-exclude
select the members to skip in the same way, and win over
.IR member s.
.PP
Each operand must also be the name of a member.
After the whole archive has been read, the first operand that is not
the name of a member that was listed or extracted, and does not match
an exclusion pattern, is reported as
.IB name ": not found in archive"
and the status is 1.
This includes an operand with a wildcard, and an operand that names a
directory for which the archive has no member, even though such an
operand selected members, and they were processed.
.SS Creating
.B \-c
adds each
.I file
to the archive, and a directory with everything below it, unless
.B \-\-no\-recursion
is given.
The names from
.B \-T
come before the operands.
With no names at all,
.B tar
fails with
.BR "empty archive" .
.PP
A member is named as the file was named on the command line, with a
trailing
.B /
removed, and with
.B /
appended for a directory.
Leading slashes, and everything through the last
.B ../
component, are removed, with the warning
.BI "removing leading '" prefix "' from member names"
printed once.
Nothing else changes: a leading
.BR ./ ,
a backslash, or a drive letter stays in the name.
A name that is
.B ..\&
or ends in
.B /..\&
leaves nothing, so that directory is not stored itself, though the files
below it are.
The files below a directory are in the order the file system lists
them.
A bare drive such as
.B c:
names the current directory of that drive.
It is stored as
.BR c:/ ,
and the files below it are named
.BR c:name .
.PP
A member records the file's type, permissions, owner, modification time
(in whole seconds), and size, followed by the contents.
A file with a link count above 1 is stored in full under the first name
found, and the later names are stored as hard link members that name the
first.
A symbolic link or junction is stored as a link, with a target that uses
forward slashes, unless
.B \-h
is given.
A junction's target is its absolute path.
A file that becomes shorter while it is archived ends the command with
.BR "short read" ,
and leaves a damaged archive.
.PP
The archive is in GNU format.
A name or link target of 100 bytes or more is stored in an extra GNU
long name member.
A size of 8 GiB or more is stored in base-256.
The archive ends with two blocks of zeros, and is not padded to a
multiple of 10240 bytes.
An archive file that is among the files being archived is skipped with
the message
.BR "file is the archive; skipping" .
.PP
The patterns of
.B \-X
and
.B \-\-exclude
are matched with
.B *
and
.B ?\&
not matching
.BR / ,
against the stored name of each file, and against the part of it after
each
.BR / .
A match skips the file, and for a directory everything below it, so
.B \-\-exclude=.git
skips every directory named
.BR .git ,
and
.B \-\-exclude=\(aq*.o\(aq
every file ending in
.BR .o .
A pattern that begins with
.B /
never matches.
.SS Listing
.B \-t
prints the name of each selected member on a line of its own, as stored,
so a directory usually ends in
.BR / .
With
.BR \-v ,
or a second
.BR \-t ,
each line holds the permissions in the form used by
.BR "ls \-l" ,
the user and group IDs separated by a slash, the size right-aligned in 9
columns, the modification time as local time in the form
.IR "YYYY\-MM\-DD hh:mm:ss" ,
and the name.
A symbolic link or hard link is followed by
.B \->
and its target.
User and group names in the archive are not used.
Leading slashes and
.B ../
components are removed from the names, as when extracting.
.SS Extracting
.B \-x
creates each selected member under its name, relative to the current
directory, or the one that
.B \-C
names.
Missing parent directories are created.
Leading slashes and everything through the last
.B ../
component are removed from member names, and from the targets of hard
links, with the warning given for creating.
Backslashes and drive letters are not recognized, so a member named
.BR C:/dir/file ,
.BR \edir\efile ,
or
.B ..\ex
is extracted where the name says, even outside the
.B \-C
directory.
List an archive from an untrusted source with
.B \-t
first.
.PP
Before a member other than a directory is created, an existing file of
the same name is removed, even if it is read-only, unless
.B \-k
or
.B \-\-overwrite
is given.
Failing to remove it, as for an existing directory, ends the command.
.PP
By type:
.IP \(bu 2
A regular file is created with the archived contents.
It is read-only if the archived permissions lack the owner write bit,
and is never made executable.
Its modification time is restored, unless
.B \-m
is given.
.IP \(bu 2
A directory is created, and an existing one is left alone.
An error creating it is reported without ending the command.
Its modification time is restored, even if it already existed, unless
.B \-m
is given, but creating files in it afterwards changes the time again.
.IP \(bu 2
A symbolic link is created as a Windows symbolic link, which requires
Developer Mode or administrator rights, with backslashes for slashes in
its target.
It is a directory link only if its target is an existing directory when
the link is made.
A link whose target begins with
.B /
or contains
.BR .. ,
or that cannot be made at once, is made after all other members, and
failing then ends the command with
.BR "can't create symlink" .
.IP \(bu 2
A hard link is made after all other members, and needs a file system
that supports hard links, such as NTFS.
Failing ends the command with
.BR "can't create hardlink" .
.IP \(bu 2
A device, FIFO, or other special file cannot be created on Windows.
It is reported as
.B "can't create node"
and skipped, without changing the exit status.
.PP
A link made after all other members, symbolic or hard, is not made when
a directory in its own name is missing, is a symbolic link or junction,
or is not a directory: that fails with
.BR "Not a directory" ,
so an archive cannot place a link through a symbolic link that it
created earlier.
.PP
Members that carry GNU long names and links, and POSIX extended headers
with a
.B path
or
.B linkpath
record, are understood; other records are ignored.
Old archives with no
.B ustar
signature are accepted.
A volume header is skipped with a warning, and any other member type,
such as a GNU sparse file, ends the command with
.BR "unknown typeflag" .
Reading stops at the first two consecutive zero blocks, and anything
after them is ignored.
An archive that is cut off at a block boundary, without them, is
accepted.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-xvf .
An option's argument may be attached, as in
.BR \-Cdir ,
or be the next argument.
Long options begin with
.BR \-\- ,
may be abbreviated to a unique prefix, and take their argument after
.B =
or as the next argument.
Options may follow the operands, unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
An unknown option prints a usage message on standard error, and the
status is 1.
Options described as accepted are recognized for compatibility, and do
nothing here.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-create
Create an archive of the
.I file
operands and the names from
.BR \-T .
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-extract
Extract the selected members.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-list
List the selected members.
.TP
\fB\-f\fR \fIfile\fR, \fB\-\-file=\fR\fIfile\fR
Read the archive from, or with
.B \-c
write it to,
.IR file .
A
.I file
of
.B \-
means standard input or output, which is the default.
The archive is opened before
.B \-C
takes effect, so a relative name refers to the directory
.B tar
started in.
.TP
\fB\-C\fR \fIdir\fR, \fB\-\-directory=\fR\fIdir\fR
Change to
.IR dir ,
which must exist, before archiving or extracting.
This applies to the
.I file
operands, but not to
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-T ,
or
.BR \-X ,
which are read first.
Wherever
.B \-C
stands, it acts once at the start, and only the last one counts.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Print the name of each member as it is added by
.BR \-c ,
on standard output, or on standard error when the archive goes to
standard output, and as it is extracted by
.BR \-x ,
on standard output, or on standard error with
.BR \-O .
With
.BR \-t ,
or a second
.B \-v
with
.BR \-x ,
print the long listing instead.
More than two have no further effect, and a second
.B \-v
with
.B \-c
changes nothing.
.TP
.BR \-O ", " \-\-to\-stdout
Write the contents of the selected members to standard output, one
after another, and create no files.
Names printed by
.B \-v
go to standard error instead.
With
.BR \-t ,
the contents are written as well as the listing, which goes to standard
error.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-touch
With
.BR \-x ,
do not restore modification times, so that extracted files and
directories get the time of extraction.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-no\-same\-owner
Accepted.
Ownership cannot be set on Windows, so it is never restored.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-same\-permissions
Accepted.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-keep\-old
Do not replace existing files.
If a regular file member names an existing file,
.B tar
reports
.BI "can't open '" name "': File exists"
and exits with status 1 at once, rather than skipping the member.
.TP
.BR \-Z ", " \-\-compress
Compress the archive with
.BR compress ,
or decompress it with
.BR uncompress .
.TP
.BR \-z ", " \-\-gzip
Compress the archive with
.BR gzip ,
or decompress it with
.BR gunzip .
.TP
.BR \-J ", " \-\-xz
Compress the archive with
.BR xz ,
or decompress it with
.BR unxz .
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-bzip2
Compress the archive with
.BR bzip2 ,
or decompress it with
.BR bunzip2 .
.TP
.B \-\-lzma
Compress the archive with
.BR lzma ,
or decompress it with
.BR unlzma .
.TP
.B \-\-zstd
Compress the archive with
.BR zstd ,
or decompress it with
.BR unzstd .
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-auto\-compress
With
.B \-c
and a file named by
.BR \-f ,
compress according to the end of the file name, as if the matching
option were given:
.B \-z
for
.BR gz ,
.B \-j
for
.BR bz2 ,
.B \-J
for
.BR xz ,
.B \-\-lzma
for
.BR lzma ,
and
.B \-\-zstd
for
.BR zst .
There need not be a period before the ending, and the match is
case-sensitive.
Any other name is not compressed.
Has no effect with
.B \-t
and
.BR \-x .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-dereference
With
.BR \-c ,
store the file or directory that a symbolic link or junction refers to,
instead of the link.
.TP
\fB\-T\fR \fIfile\fR, \fB\-\-files\-from=\fR\fIfile\fR
Read names from
.IR file ,
one per line, and use them as if they were operands that come before
those on the command line.
With
.B \-c
they are the files to archive, and otherwise the
.IR member s
to select.
A line may end with LF or CRLF.
A trailing
.B /
is removed, unless the name is only
.BR / .
A
.I file
of
.B \-
means standard input.
May be repeated.
.TP
\fB\-X\fR \fIfile\fR, \fB\-\-exclude\-from=\fR\fIfile\fR
Read wildcard patterns from
.IR file ,
one per line, with the same rules as
.BR \-T ,
and skip the files or members that match.
May be repeated.
.TP
\fB\-\-exclude=\fR\fIpattern\fR
Skip the files or members that match
.IR pattern ,
like a line of
.BR \-X .
A trailing
.B /
is kept.
May be repeated.
.TP
.B \-\-overwrite
With
.BR \-x ,
replace an existing file by truncating and writing it, instead of
removing it first.
This fails with
.B Permission denied
for a read-only file.
.TP
\fB\-\-strip\-components=\fR\fInumber\fR
With
.BR \-x ,
remove
.I number
leading components from the names of the members.
.I number
is a decimal integer, and 0 removes nothing.
A member that has no more components than
.IR number ,
such as the top directory when
.I number
is 1, is skipped.
The target of a hard link is shortened the same way, and that of a
symbolic link is not.
Operands and
.B \-v
output use the names as stored.
No effect with
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-t ,
and
.BR \-O .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-recursion
With
.BR \-c ,
archive a directory without its contents.
.TP
.B \-\-numeric\-owner
Accepted.
This build never looks up user and group names.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-same\-permissions
Accepted.
A file is still made read-only when its archived permissions lack the
owner write bit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
As the first argument, print a usage message on standard output and
exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
As the first argument, print
.B tar (busybox)
and the busybox version on standard output and exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the command line are expanded at startup against the files
in the current directory, even if quoted, for callers such as
.B cmd.exe
that do not expand them, so a
.I member
pattern such as
.B *.c
may become a list of file names.
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
patterns as the shell passed them.
.TP
.B BB_UMASK
An octal file mode creation mask applied at startup.
With
.BR \-c ,
its group and other write bits are removed from the stored permissions.
If it includes the owner write bit,
.BR 0200 ,
the archive file created by
.B \-f
and the files created by
.B \-x
are read-only.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
If the first of these that is set has the value
.BR C ,
listings and the names printed by
.B \-v
show each byte of a non-ASCII character as
.BR ? ;
see
.BR NOTES .
.TP
.B PATH
Where the compression programs are found.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
If set, options must precede the operands.
.TP
.B TZ
The time zone of the times printed by the long listing.
The format is that of the Microsoft C runtime, such as
.BR EST5EDT .
When it is unset, the Windows time zone setting applies.
.PP
Output to a console also depends on
.BR BB_TERMINAL_MODE ,
.BR BB_SKIP_ANSI_EMULATION ,
.BR LC_ALL ,
.BR LC_CTYPE ,
and
.BR LANG ,
as for all busybox applets.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including when problems were reported and skipped: a special
file that could not be created, or a directory that could not be made.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid command line, with a usage message on standard error, or any
other error: a file that cannot be read, created, or removed, a damaged
or empty archive, a compression program that cannot be started or that
fails when creating, or an operand that is not found in the archive.
.TP
.B 141
Writing to a pipe whose reader has exited, such as in
.BR "tar tf big.tar | head \-1" ,
or when a compression program has failed at once.
There is no message.
.PP
Errors are reported on standard error as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
tar: \fImessage\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
When creating, an error with one file does not stop the others.
.B tar
writes the archive anyway, reports
.BR "error exit delayed from previous errors" ,
and exits with status 1.
When extracting, an error ends the command at once, except for those
that leave the status 0.
.SH EXAMPLES
Archive a directory, compressed with gzip:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ tar czf src.tgz src
.EE
.RE
.PP
Do the same with zstd, picked by the name:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ tar caf src.tar.zst src
.EE
.RE
.PP
List an archive in detail:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ tar tvf src.tgz
drwxrwxr\-x 4095/4095         0 2026\-10\-19 21:18:08 src/
\-rw\-rw\-r\-\- 4095/4095         6 2026\-10\-19 21:18:08 src/a.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Extract into an existing directory:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ tar xf src.tgz \-C c:/build
.EE
.RE
.PP
Extract a source release without its top directory:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ mkdir pkg
$ tar xf pkg\-1.0.tar.xz \-\-strip\-components=1 \-C pkg
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print one file from an archive:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ tar xOf src.tgz src/a.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Archive a directory without object files and version control data:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ tar cf src.tar \-\-exclude=\(aq*.o\(aq \-\-exclude=.git src
.EE
.RE
.PP
Archive the files that
.BR find (1)
lists:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ find . \-name \(aq*.c\(aq | tar cf src.tar \-T \-
.EE
.RE
.PP
Copy a directory tree:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ tar cf \- src | (cd dest && tar xf \-)
.EE
.RE
.PP
Compress with a chosen level, which
.B tar
cannot pass on:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ tar cf \- src | zstd \-19 >src.tar.zst
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU
.BR tar :
.IP \(bu 2
There are no append, update, compare, or delete modes, and no
multi-volume, sparse file, or incremental support.
Options such as
.BR \-r ,
.BR \-u ,
.BR \-A ,
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-P ,
.BR \-b ,
.BR \-\-transform ,
and
.B \-\-to\-command
are invalid.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-k
fails on an existing file instead of skipping it.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-o
means only
.BR \-\-no\-same\-owner ,
even when creating.
.IP \(bu 2
When listing and extracting,
.IR member s
and the patterns of
.B \-X
and
.B \-\-exclude
are compared with only the leading components of a name, so
.B \-\-exclude=\(aq*.txt\(aq
skips only top-level names.
Use
.B \-\-exclude=\(aq*/*.txt\(aq
for files one level down.
When creating, the patterns are compared with every component, and skip
such files anywhere.
.IP \(bu 2
An operand with a wildcard selects members, but is then reported as not
found, and the status is 1.
.IP \(bu 2
Archives are written in the GNU format, with no option for POSIX or
other formats.
.IP \(bu 2
Listings show numeric user and group IDs, and extraction ignores the
names in the archive.
.PP
Names are stored and extracted byte for byte: UTF-8 on Windows 10
version 1903 and later, and the system code page on earlier versions.
Backslashes are not converted to slashes.
Listings, and the names printed by
.BR \-v ,
show each control character as
.BR ? .
On Windows 10 version 1903 and later, they show non-ASCII characters,
and each byte that is not part of a valid UTF-8 sequence as
.BR ? ,
unless the first of
.BR LC_ALL ,
.BR LC_CTYPE ,
and
.B LANG
that is set has the value
.BR C ,
which shows each byte of a non-ASCII character as
.BR ? .
A name that Windows rejects, such as one with
.B *
or
.BR ? ,
or a device name such as
.BR con ,
ends the command with an error when extracting.
So does a path longer than
.BR MAX_PATH ,
260 characters, counting the current directory.
.PP
When creating, the stored attributes come from busybox-w32's emulation
of Unix file information:
.IP \(bu 2
Everyone may read, and write unless the file is read-only, less the
group and other write bits of the umask.
The default umask is 002, which removes the write bit for others.
Directories, and files whose names end in
.BR .bat ,
.BR .cmd ,
.BR .com ,
.BR .exe ,
or
.BR .sh ,
or that begin with
.B #!\&
or are PE executables other than DLLs, are executable.
.IP \(bu 2
A file owned by the current user has user and group ID 4095, and the
owner and group names stored are the user's name.
For a file of another owner, the ID is the relative ID of the owner's
account when that is from 500 to 4094, and the name is that number.
Otherwise the ID is 0, and the name is
.BR root .
.IP \(bu 2
Hard links are recognized by the file index and the volume.
.IP \(bu 2
The modification time is the last write time.
A time before 1970 is stored as 0.
.PP
When extracting, the owner write permission is the only one that has an
effect: a file without it is made read-only.
Directories are never read-only, and nothing is made executable.
The owner is never restored.
